aph\\\":{\\\"format_version\\\":2,\\\"case_id\\\":\\\"wave-map-energy\\\",\\\"spec_sha256\\\":\\\"controller-bound\\\",\\\"propositions\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H001\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For all x∈R^n and r>0, |u(x,r)|=1.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H002\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For all x∈R^n,r>0 there exists a∈R such that u_tt(x,r)-Δ_xu(x,r)=a u(x,r), with componentwise Δ_x.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"H003\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For each compact interval J⊂(0,∞), there is compact C_J⊂R^n such that u(x,r)=q for x∉C_J and r∈J.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"q\\\"]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"target\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"For every r>0, e(·,r) is Lebesgue integrable, E is differentiable at r, and E'(r)=0.\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\"]}],\\\"inferences\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R001\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"output\\\":\\\"P\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\",\\\"D003\\\"],\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\"],\\\"locals\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"r0\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"positive real number\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S006\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"arbitrary\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r0>0.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[]},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"J\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"compact interval in (0,∞) with r0 in its interior\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S006\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"witness\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Choose J after the existence fact F001.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[\\\"F001\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"]},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"compact subset of R^n\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S006\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"witness\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Choose the compact set supplied by H003 for J.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"from_local\\\":[\\\"J\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"J\\\"]},{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"B\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"closed coordinate box containing C_J\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R001/argument\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S003\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S006\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"witness\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Choose B after the existence fact F002.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[\\\"F002\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"C_J\\\"]}],\\\"argument\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r0>0. Choose δ with 0<δ<r0 and set J=[r0−δ,r0+δ]; thus there exists a compact interval in (0,∞) with r0 in its interior, recorded as F001.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"F001\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"There exists a compact interval J⊂(0,∞) with r0 in its interior.\\\",\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\"],\\\"assumptions\\\":[]}],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S002\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Choose J using F001. H003 applied to this compact interval supplies compact C_J and the stated uniform equality. Compactness of C_J implies boundedness, so a closed coordinate box B containing it exists; record this as F002. On the open complement of C_J, u=q for all times in J, so its spatial and time first derivatives vanish there. By continuity they vanish also at boundary points of C_J. Hence e(·,s) is continuous, zero off C_J and bounded on C_J, and therefore Lebesgue integrable for every s∈J.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[\\\"F001\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"J\\\",\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"B\\\",\\\"q\\\",\\\"u\\\",\\\"e\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"F002\\\",\\\"statement\\\":\\\"There exists a closed coordinate box B⊂R^n containing C_J.\\\",\\\"binders\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"n\\\"],\\\"assumptions\\\":[]}],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S003\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Choose B by F002. For s∈J, E(s)=∫_B e(x,s)dx since e vanishes outside C_J⊂B. On B×J, e_s is continuous and uniformly continuous. For sufficiently small nonzero h with r0+h∈J, the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus gives (e(x,r0+h)−e(x,r0))/h=∫_0^1 e_s(x,r0+θh)dθ. Uniform continuity implies these quotients converge uniformly in x∈B to e_s(x,r0). B has finite measure, so the difference between their integrals and ∫_B e_s(x,r0)dx is at most measure(B) times the uniform error, tending to zero. Thus E is differentiable at r0 and E'(r0)=∫_B e_s(x,r0)dx. Differentiating the finite sum gives e_s=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_j u_j·∂_j u_t, where u_j=∂_j u.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[\\\"F002\\\"],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"B\\\",\\\"J\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"u\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S004\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"For each coordinate j, f_j(x)=u_t(x,r0)·u_j(x,r0) is smooth and supported in C_J. Its derivative ∂_j f_j=(∂_j u_t)·u_j+u_t·u_jj is continuous and compactly supported, hence absolutely integrable. Choose a coordinate interval containing C_J in its interior. For each fixed choice of the other coordinates, the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus gives integral in coordinate j of ∂_j f_j equal to the endpoint difference of f_j, which is zero. Fubini is applicable because ∂_j f_j is absolutely integrable; iterating the integral gives ∫_{R^n}∂_j f_j=0. Therefore ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_j=−∫u_t·u_jj. Summing the finite set of identities yields E'(r0)=2∫_{R^n}u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)dx.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_j\\\",\\\"u_jj\\\",\\\"n\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\",\\\"E\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S005\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Differentiating |u(x,r)|²=1 in time gives u·u_t=0. By H002, at each x there is a real a with u_tt−Δ_xu=a u. Consequently u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0 pointwise, so the integral expression for E'(r0) is zero.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"u\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_tt\\\",\\\"Δ_x\\\",\\\"a\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S006\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Since r0 was arbitrary, the integrability, differentiability, and vanishing derivative established at r0 hold for every positive time. No assertion at the endpoint zero is used.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[]}]}}\"}","status":"completed"}}

